
= 6 Conclusions and limitations

*The count (Q1, Q2).* For the Blocksworld states there is no single right dimension, and none of the ones we fixed before training predicted the count across settings. On the 36 facts at β = 1 the rate stays near 8 nats while the count moves, so the count is just how many units share it, which does not tell us why it settles where it does. The learning rate in one network, the encoding and, in a single-seed pilot, the warm-up move the count, the last in line with Sønderby et al. @sonderby2016ladder. Dropping the derived facts brings the count close to code10, as the information hypothesis predicted, although we saw this only after the main results and at one network.

Copied bits get informative codes, as the reconstruction–KL balance suggests, but the balance does not say how many units these codes use, and in the 2×256 network the objective does not prefer 15 units over 8 to 10. In the reference settings the median count stays at 15 as the latents grow from 24 to 64, as Burda et al. found @burda2016iwae, which fits Asperti's suggestion of an internal dimension for a given architecture @asperti2018sparsity[p. 1]. However, as Asperti and Trentin also found @asperti2020balancing, the count moves with training choices, so we read it as a property of the states together with the network, the encoding and the training.

*The units (Q3).* In the reference configuration most active units detect one or two facts, mostly a block on another and the reverse at opposite ends of their range, so they are tied to facts but not one to one, and similar counts occur with very different codes.

*Sampling (Q4).* The β-VAEs of the main sweep at β = 0.25 and 1 reconstruct every state from their posterior means, but their prior samples are often illegal, as the samples of Asperti's VAE trained on images of one to three tiles often had more tiles @asperti2018sparsity[pp. 5–6]. The gap to aggregate-posterior samples fits the prior mismatch, which Asperti indicates as the problem when reconstruction is good but generation is bad @asperti2019collapse[p. 7], but we did not test it. As a next experiment we would keep the trained decoder and fit a Gaussian mixture to the posterior means of the training states @ghosh2020rae @asperti2021survey, or a second VAE as in the two-stage VAE of @dai2019diagnosing, which he also suggests for this case. We would then check whether its samples are more often legal than those from the prior.

*Limitations.* Our results hold for the small networks, data and encodings we used, and they come from coarse comparisons over a few seeds. The follow-up used one network and learning rate, and on activations we had one transformer at each extreme of width. Finally, the analyses we chose after the main results reuse test data we had already seen.
